WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:08.721
Stolen X R P cannot be pulled back, but a lot of X R P is simply stuck, waiting for its owner to sign one transaction.

2
00:00:08.721 --> 00:00:13.479
And most thefts start with one signature the owner did not understand.

3
00:00:15.179 --> 00:00:17.805
A matured escrow does not pay out on its own.

4
00:00:17.805 --> 00:00:19.147
Someone must finish it.

5
00:00:19.597 --> 00:00:22.434
A check written to you pays nothing until you cash it.

6
00:00:22.884 --> 00:00:27.126
And every object an account owns locks zero point two X R P.

7
00:00:27.126 --> 00:00:30.095
Removing ones you no longer need frees it.

8
00:00:30.545 --> 00:00:38.267
A real example: Ripple's escrow wallet number three holds ten escrows to itself, each waiting for a finishing transaction.

9
00:00:39.967 --> 00:00:43.548
A phishing site does not need your seed if you will sign for it.

10
00:00:43.548 --> 00:00:47.519
Set regular key and signer list set hand signing power to someone else.

11
00:00:47.969 --> 00:00:50.743
Account delete sends everything to its destination.

12
00:00:51.193 --> 00:00:54.776
And an N F T sell offer for zero gives the token away.

13
00:00:54.776 --> 00:00:57.364
Decode every request before signing it.

14
00:00:57.364 --> 00:01:00.814
The Security Center's free Pre-sign check does this.

15
00:01:02.514 --> 00:01:05.661
Anyone can write a website name on an account.

16
00:01:05.661 --> 00:01:12.502
The claim is proven only when that website lists the account back in its x r p ledger dot toml file.

17
00:01:12.502 --> 00:01:16.811
X R P Toolkit's donations account does both, so it is verified.

18
00:01:16.811 --> 00:01:19.069
The free Domain check tests this.
